  "query_preview": "..Plenty of clouds have lingered around through the \nmorning due to a mesoscale convective vortex (MCV) associated \nwith a few embedded thundershowers at times over Cochise, \nGraham, and Greenlee counties. The lingering MCV has presented a\nunique forecast challenge for the afternoon. Clouds and \nthundershowers earlier today could inhibit the growth of \ninstability across favored areas for thunderstorm development \nthis afternoon and evening (i.e., White Mountains, Chiricahuas, \nand perhaps the Pinalenos and even Mount Lemmon). However, there\nis still plenty of time left in the day for instability to grow\nand overcome the cap as the MCV continues to move west and \nsouth.\n\nOvernight tonight there is uncertainty on whether we see another \nround of lingering showers and storms (perhaps in the form of \nanother MCV south and east of Tucson. The NBM and a few of the \n01/12Z REFS members (notably the HRRR and REFS-2) have latched onto \nthis solution so far across portions of Cochise and Santa Cruz \ncounties overnight into the early morning hours. The NBM has PoPs \novernight in the 50-70% range for Cochise County, but I bumped them \ndown into the 20-50%) range and expanded a slight chance (10-20%) \nwest into Santa Cruz County. This outcome is highly dependent on how \nstorms evolve in New Mexico this evening but still worth \nhighlighting.\n\nTomorrow, another day of similar northeasterly flow aloft will \nsupport storm chances (20-40%) again mainly in the White Mountains \nand areas east and south of Tucson. In the early evening I could see \nthere potentially being a shower or storm in the Tucson Metro based \non a weak but nonzero signal for divergence aloft. However, my \nconfidence remains rather low at this time.\n\nMonday, the midlevel high is becoming more elongated. There is \nincreasing confidence for storms to be able to push a bit further \nwest toward the Tucson Metro. But it still looks like the best \ndeformation and thus strongest lift and storms are going to set up \nin the afternoon over Sonora, Mexico. What is notable about Monday, \nhowever, is the chance for perhaps some nocturnal activity \nassociated with an MCS south of Tucson along the International \nBorder. This solution is currently supported by the latest 01/12Z U \nof A WRF-GFS. At the very least, thunderstorm outflows in northern \nMexico could send a reinforcing moisture surge into southeast \nArizona as we head into what looks like perhaps an even more active \nperiod in the middle to latter half of the week. The gradual uptick \nin moisture each day will cause the threat for localized flash \nflooding later in the week.",
